# Bagirathi's Wager
Tamil title: பாகீரதியின் பந்தயம்

## Image 1: 001-5db1bbbc26.png

### Bagirathi's Wager

Presented here is a translation of a story written by C.N.A. in *Homeland*.

[He was only an attendant, but he worked in the house of one of the Congress party's “big men.” Only men like him could know the true, undisguised form of those “big men.”

Here is one day from his experience.]

My master rose before dawn, bathed, and went into his private room.

“Do not let anyone in without my permission, whoever comes.” That was the strict order he gave me.

I was startled, because he had never said such a thing before.

Whoever came, he would speak sweetly, chat with them, and pass the time until no one noticed how many hours had gone by. Passing the time in that fashion was one of his daily duties.

He would talk about all manner of things, and many people would be struck down in his conversation.

“Ministers, they call themselves! What do these ministers ever accomplish? They remain buried in files all day. Their work ends with orders to move the comma here over there, bring the semicolon there over here, and revise a sentence in some particular way.

## Image 2: 002-a574777d97.png

“What else do they accomplish? How can the party grow if things go on like this? Men like me have to intervene, set matters right, do what must be done, meet the people who must be met, say things tactfully, and make the party grow. A party has to be run by patting some people on the back, lifting others onto one's shoulders and praising them, tempting still others, and drawing every one of them in.

“Had I not intervened, that Polanath would have left the party by now, causing it a tremendous loss.

“If not for that same Polanath, could we have arranged a six-course feast for the minister, the deputy minister who came from Delhi, and gladdened his heart? Polanath had apparently asked the minister for something, and the minister refused. Naturally, Polanath flew into a rage. The minister's explanation was that the law did not permit it. Is that not a remarkably amusing explanation? Ministers have been seated on those pedestals precisely to make laws, and are paid enormous salaries for that very purpose.

“Ah, what I endured to pacify the furious Polanath! Only after arguing with him for hours and singing his praises as a mountain of virtue and a noble lord did his anger subside.

“Take Karim Khan. What have I not done to bring that Muslim League man into our party? A man who would not even look in the direction of Congress now has khadi adorning his body. How did that happen? It was all my skill!”

My master would often gather four people around him and measure out tales of his glory and prowess in this fashion.

How could I not be surprised when that very man ordered me not to admit anyone?

## Image 3: 003-b15ecd35bb.png

I sat in my chair outside the room. The door was closed.

Pot-bellied Parangusam arrived. That was how my master had taken to describing him lately, but only in his absence. Would he dare say it when Parangusam was present?

I stopped him.

“What insolence! Just watch what I do to you!” Parangusam roared.

“Sir, forgive me, sir! Those are the master's orders, sir. What can I do?” I pleaded.

“The master's orders! Who is the master here? That fellow inside, that dimwit? We provide his food and clothes. How can he be my master? He is your master, and that is all. Understand? Cross my path because he told you to, and I will crack your skull. Be careful!” He sprang at me as he spoke.

“Sir, sir, let me go!” I cried.

Fortunately, my master noticed the commotion from inside the room.

“Parangusam! What is this? Are you displaying your rowdyism here?”

“Oh! Since when did you become such a great man? I was wrong. Look, I struck him when I should have struck you. Just wait and see what I do.”

“Master, shall I call the police?” I asked.

“Do not panic. Why call the police? Parangusam behaves like this when he is drunk.”

“I am drunk? Yes, I am. So what? You took two hundred rupees from me to obtain that permit! The law is on my side. And besides, look here, I have this walking stick too.”

## Image 4: 004-285948c216.png

“All right, all right. Tell me what the matter is. Why have you come now? I have no time and a great deal of work. Speak quickly.”

“Why was I not selected as the party's candidate?”

“Oh! You were not selected? I have only just learned of it. I am delighted!”

“Delighted?”

“Yes. What else should an ungrateful man receive? Last month I came to you for a small favour, and that too for the party, but you brushed me aside. You got what you deserved. I am very pleased. The Selection Board rejected you, did it not? To tell the truth, I was foolish enough to recommend your name, and not for your sake either. It was for Bagirathi's sake, out of regard for her. What could I do? The board knows far too much about you.”

“What? You recommended me, and the board rejected me? Is that what happened?”

“Yes. The board has complete authority.”

“Why did this board behave so stupidly? Why is it so arrogant?”

“Ask the members of the board, if you can get near them. Better still, ask Soori.”

“Soori? That black marketeer? What has he to do with this?”

“How would you know? I should not reveal the secret to you, but I shall tell you because of our old association. Soori is going to become chairman.”

“Soori, chairman? It will not happen. It can never happen!”

“After setting aside ten lakh rupees for the purpose, how could it fail? He has promised to help every candidate standing in the election.

## Image 5: 005-a9f3057f70.png

“He has imposed two conditions. Do you know what they are? First, you must be sent out. Second, he must be made chairman.”

“What did all of you say to that? Did you agree?”

“I did not, but the board gave its consent. What else could it do? The party must win somehow. I still had faith in you, so I went to the higher authorities and recommended you. But you ruined it yourself. You said that you could not provide financial assistance to the party's candidates. I therefore had to tell the board to make some other arrangement, and that is precisely what it did. Even now, nothing has been completely lost. Soori's application is still in my hands; it has not reached the board.”

“Think calmly for a moment. Do not rush. Last time I gave the party a great deal of money, and it secured a good majority.”

“You were chairman. You received an excellent opportunity to mix with many people. Did you not? More than a dozen ministers dined with you and shook your hand.”

“Are you saying that this balances the five lakh rupees I spent?”

“I am not much of an accountant, but I understand matters to some extent and can argue them. It is true that you spent five lakhs, but did you take that money out of your own pocket? How many sums of five lakhs must you have earned with my help? Can you deny it? Why debate all that now? You enjoyed the office. Now you say you do not want it. What is the use of blaming the board afterward?”

## Image 6: 006-671e1bd68f.png

“You know that Soori and I are fierce business rivals. What will happen to me if his influence and status grow?”

“What can I do about that? I truly feel sorry when I look at you, but the party cannot attend to all such matters.”

“Then they have decided to destroy me. Is that it?”

“I would not put it that way. The party will not forgo the assistance it can obtain from Soori. The result may be harmful to you, but what of the party?”

“You are vultures, carrion-eating vultures!”

“The same mouth that once called us thieves now calls us vultures. One name when you are pleased and another when you are distressed, is that not so, Parangusam?”

“Soori has entered the contracting business.”

“Until now, you were the uncrowned king of that field. You amassed money by the heap through it. Can you deny that?”

“It is true that I made a profit. But did I fail to give you a share?”

“You gave us a little, I admit.”

“Then why did you bring Soori into the middle of it?”

“Me? The board had to find someone, whether Soori or Poori. Once you spread your hands and said you could not help, was the party to sit in a corner with its hands tied? The party must win somehow and must be made to grow. What other course was there but to draw Soori in?”

## Image 7: 007-9a57a64a96.png

“Then all your claims that the party's influence had grown and that victory was certain were lies?”

“I said it then, and I say it now. It is because our influence has grown that the moment Parangusam leaves, Soori steps in. Could that happen otherwise?”

“Oh! Is that the influence you meant? I thought you were speaking of the people's support.”

“When we have so many shorter routes available, why worry about principles?”

“Many of you proclaimed that all the people's support was with you and that victory was therefore certain. What of all that?”

“It was correct to say victory was certain. The reason given for it may have been wrong. So what? In their enthusiasm, they may have exaggerated a little.”

“Why do you confuse the matter? Your party has lost the people's support. To compensate for it, you draw in men like Soori and drain their money to purchase support for the party. Is that not the truth?”

“This time you occupy the position Soori once occupied. You did not understand the truth then; only now has it become clear to you, has it not?”

“You merciless people! Your arrogance grows by the day. Do you imagine that the people do not know of your shortcuts, crooked schemes, and improper acts?”

“Oh! Have you been reading the DMK newspapers? Judging from the way you speak, that is how it sounds to me.”

“You know that I do not read newspapers at all. I scarcely have time to look at my account books; where would I find time for newspapers? I am merely telling you the truth openly, without concealment.”

## Image 8: 008-6735a39d89.png

“It may be true, but it is not the whole truth. When one is in power, one must retain power by whatever means possible. We pursue whatever course is needed. You cannot spend enough money, but it appears Soori can.”

“It only appears so. Soori conducts all kinds of trickery, like the Birlas and Bajajs. One day he will be caught in danger. You will see.”

“But when the board asked him, he agreed without the least hesitation.”

“He agreed merely for appearance's sake, to satisfy you. I know for certain that he cannot spend that much.”

“Wrong! In these three months alone he is apparently going to export goods worth forty lakh rupees. I do not know what commodity, but it is true that he is exporting.”

“Pooh! Only forty lakhs? I export goods worth nearly one crore.”

“Do you possess the required order?”

“Certainly!”

“I know nothing about these matters, but it is possible that some change may occur in that order.”

“Do not frighten me needlessly!”

“No, no. Why would I frighten you? You were saying something about exports and imports. You know that I take no interest at all in business. That is why I mentioned it.”

“All right. If your scheme is to go so far as to tighten around my neck, then I must think.”

“Shall I hold back Soori's application for a while, then?”

## Image 9: 009-fc73bdf5d6.png

“Do not send it at all. Give me one week. I shall tell you my decision.”

“A week? How is that possible? The board keeps troubling me, and the DMK attacks me day after day. I ordered that no one be admitted precisely to escape the man coming from the board. Had Bagirathi not intervened, Soori's application would have reached the board by now. She insists that the office must not leave your hands.”

“Then Bagirathi knows something of Soori's plan.”

“Something? She knows all of it. Do you know that Soori is trying to become chairman in order to humiliate Bagirathi?”

“What has Bagirathi to do with this?”

“Do you truly know nothing? Bagirathi adores you and always speaks highly of you. One day she met Shoba, Soori's wife. As usual, Bagirathi began praising you. Shoba interrupted her: ‘What is difficult about it? Anyone can become chairman by spending money.’ Bagirathi denied it, and a wager was made. Do you know its terms? She challenged Shoba: ‘If Parangusam alone fails to become chairman, slap me publicly on the cheek, and I shall accept it.’”

“Good God! Has the matter gone that far? I knew nothing of it.”

“Soori has no liking for politics. He is now throwing himself into it only to fulfil his wife's wish. Bagirathi has become frightened. She was saying that she wanted to meet you alone and speak with you.”

## Image 10: 010-23565fcb2a.png

“I saw her recently, at the exhibition.”

“Saw her? Did you expect her to run up to you at once, especially in such a place? She is deeply distressed. It was she who asked me not to send Soori's proposal to the board.”

“Shoba slap Bagirathi? Impossible! It shall never happen. Her cheeks are not meant to be slapped! Ah, yes. I have been a fool all these days. Tear up Soori's proposal. I am ready to become chairman again.”

“Truly? Then I must leave for Madras immediately. When is the first train?”

“Take my car. There must be no delay. Before you leave, meet Bagirathi and tell her my decision.”

“Bagirathi will leap for joy. She may even say that she wants to come with us.”

“Why, would it not be better if I came with you too?”

“Excellent. We shall leave at exactly eight.”

*Dravida Nadu*, 15 March 1959.
